{"name":"Whale Tail","hex":"#86878c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"whale tail","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An unintentional display of a thong above the waistband of trousers etc.","A kind of large automotive rear spoiler."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/whale_tail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Whale tail","blocks":[{"text":"Whale tail is the Y-shaped rear portion of a thong or G-string when visible above the waistline of low-rise trousers, shorts, or skirts that resembles a whale's tail. The fashion was popular in the early 2000s, but declined within the decade, and by 2019, had remerged in popularity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Trosor-bakifr%C3%A5n.png/330px-Trosor-bakifr%C3%A5n.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whale_tail","wikidata":"Q1973161"},{"title":"Whale","blocks":[{"text":"Whales are a widely distributed and diverse group of fully aquatic placental marine mammals. As an informal and colloquial grouping, they correspond to large members of the infraorder Cetacea, i.e. all cetaceans apart from dolphins and porpoises. Dolphins and porpoises may be considered whales from a formal, cladistic perspective. Whales, dolphins and porpoises belong to the order Cetartiodactyla, which consists of even-toed ungulates. Their closest non-cetacean living relatives are the hippopotamuses, from which they and other cetaceans diverged about 54 million years ago. The two parvorders of whales, baleen whales (Mysticeti) and toothed whales (Odontoceti), are thought to have had their last common ancestor around 34 million years ago. Mysticetes include four extant (living) families: Balaenopteridae, Balaenidae, Cetotheriidae, and Eschrichtiidae. Odontocetes include the Monodontidae, Physeteridae, Kogiidae, and Ziphiidae, as well as the six families of dolphins and porpoises which are not considered whales in the informal sense."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Southern_right_whale.jpg/330px-Southern_right_whale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whale","wikidata":"Q1865281"},{"title":"Tail","blocks":[{"text":"The tail is the elongated section at the rear end of a bilaterian animal's body; in general, the term refers to a distinct, flexible appendage extending backwards from the midline of the torso. In vertebrate animals that evolved to lose their tails, the coccyx is the homologous vestigial of the tail. While tails are primarily considered a feature of vertebrates, some invertebrates such as scorpions and springtails, as well as snails and slugs, have tail-like appendages that are also referred to as tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/White-tailed_deer%2C_tail_up.jpg/330px-White-tailed_deer%2C_tail_up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tail","wikidata":"Q60960"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Madonna_11%2C_2012.jpg/500px-Madonna_11%2C_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Madonna_11%2C_2012.jpg/1280px-Madonna_11%2C_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Madonna_11%2C_2012.jpg/500px-Madonna_11%2C_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Recording artists such as Madonna (seen here undressing on stage at Seattle MDNA Tour in 2012), Christina Aguilera and Britney Spears made visible underwear and whale tails popular.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMadonna_11%2C_2012.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Humpback_whale_fluke_%281%29.jpg/500px-Humpback_whale_fluke_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Humpback_whale_fluke_%281%29.jpg/1280px-Humpback_whale_fluke_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Humpback_whale_fluke_%281%29.jpg/500px-Humpback_whale_fluke_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A whale's tail, also called a fluke, in whole or as one of the lobes.[64][65]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHumpback_whale_fluke_(1).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Walia_ibex_illustration_white_background.png/330px-Walia_ibex_illustration_white_background.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Walia_ibex_illustration_white_background.png/330px-Walia_ibex_illustration_white_background.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWalia_ibex_illustration_white_background.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Basilosaurus_cetoides_%281%29.jpg/500px-Basilosaurus_cetoides_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Basilosaurus_cetoides_%281%29.jpg/1280px-Basilosaurus_cetoides_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Basilosaurus_cetoides_%281%29.jpg/500px-Basilosaurus_cetoides_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Basilosaurus skeleton","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABasilosaurus_cetoides_(1).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Baleen_parts.png/500px-Baleen_parts.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Baleen_parts.png/500px-Baleen_parts.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Features of a blue whale","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABaleen_parts.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Sperm_whale_skeleton_labelled.jpg/500px-Sperm_whale_skeleton_labelled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Sperm_whale_skeleton_labelled.jpg/1280px-Sperm_whale_skeleton_labelled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Sperm_whale_skeleton_labelled.jpg/500px-Sperm_whale_skeleton_labelled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Features of a sperm whale skeleton","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASperm_whale_skeleton_labelled.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/GreenlandWhaleLyd3.jpg/500px-GreenlandWhaleLyd3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/GreenlandWhaleLyd3.jpg/500px-GreenlandWhaleLyd3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Skeleton of a bowhead whale; notice the vestigial pelvis. Richard Lydekker, 1894","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreenlandWhaleLyd3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Delfinekko.gif/330px-Delfinekko.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikiped